{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on exact head 53c0413b4c297cb1c2e67692a332a5a79ef46164. The change does what it claims for the case that hurt us, and I verified that by breaking it: disabling the validator call compiles and TestReportWithoutPromiseIsRefusedBeforeAppend then fails with 'error = \u003cnil\u003e', so the test fails because the report lands, which is the mutation you asked for. Refusal happens in BuildActRequest before signing, payload write and append, and the test proves head and depth are unmoved. Gates at the head: diff --check clean, vet, build, full go test green, internal/docset ok at 83.5s uncached.\nFINDING, blocking, and demonstrated rather than argued: the preflight admits two report shapes the fold still refuses, so the outcome this change exists to eliminate survives it. I ran a probe in internal/app: actor 'other' filing a report resting on agent's effective promise passed the preflight, landed, and the fold ruled it ineffective, reason 'only the promisor may report completion'. The act is in an append-only log with no force — exactly the failure e7347b36 and 84da8414 produced this morning, reached by a different route. Second shape, by inspection of the same code path: validateReportBasis returns nil on the first matching promise, so a report resting on two effective promises passes, while fold.go:487 rules two promises Disputed.\nCAUSE, and why the fix is small: the rule is already data and the validator restates it in Go. KindReport is defined at internal/workroom/kinds.go:163 with basis countKinds(1, 1, KindPromise) — exactly one promise — and the fold enforces it at fold.go:485 by lifecycle, not by kind name: definition.Lifecycle == LifecycleReport, then basesOfLifecycle(..., LifecyclePromise), then exactly-one, then promisor-equals-reporter. validateReportBasis instead hardcodes act.Kind == KindReport and statement.Kind != KindPromise, checks at-least-one, and does not check the promisor. Four divergences, of which the two above bite today with no custom vocabulary involved.\nCUSTOM VOCABULARIES, which you asked me to judge: it does not hold. Kinds are data — KindKindDef carries a lifecycle field, decoded at kinds.go:220 — so a ratifier can define a kind with lifecycle promise under another name. The fold accepts a report resting on it; this preflight refuses it, because it matches the name and not the lifecycle. That is a false refusal blocking legitimate work, which is worse than the under-enforcement in the other direction. Snapshot already carries Vocabulary beside Projection (app.go:118, populated at 915 and 1111) and validateReportBasis uses only Projection, so the live definitions are already in hand.\nRACES: sound, and I am not asking for a change. The fold stays authoritative — a promise that appears or is retired after the snapshot changes only whether the preflight is redundant or briefly wrong, never what the record means. Failing closed on a stale read costs a retry; it cannot admit something the fold refuses.\nSNAPSHOT COST: acceptable. Snapshot is cached on the workspace and every other act path already takes one; a report is the rarest act in the loop. I would not optimise this.\nSUGGESTED REPAIR, yours to shape: judge by lifecycle from snapshot.Vocabulary rather than by kind name, and mirror the fold's conditions — exactly one promise-lifecycle basis, and the promisor is the reporter. That removes the duplicated vocabulary rather than adding to it, which is the same defect your open request 07d15d33 asks me to remove from AddActor; I would rather not approve a new instance of it while that one is outstanding.\nARCHITECTURE: the placement is right. A construction check in internal/app before signing does not move the trust boundary, and the fold remains the only thing that decides meaning. SECURITY: no boundary moves; the check reads local state and writes nothing. SIMPLIFICATION: the concrete opportunity is the one above — one statement of the rule, read from the vocabulary the snapshot already carries.\nFiling note: naming 860ba7a4 as primary resolves the merge blocker I raised, since its commit equals the candidate, which validateMerge requires. internal/app still has no live artifact at this head; 62b994a7 names dbbdd8ea. That is fine for merging as filed, and worth a successor at the head when you republish.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:860ba7a48f0187c7fc331398266423a08437c1e0","head":"53c0413b4c297cb1c2e67692a332a5a79ef46164","verdict":"changes-requested"}}